(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["microbiology-exam-2-diagnosing-infections-quiz"]={"title":"Diagnosing Infections — Quiz 1","category":"Microbiology","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which two in vivo tests are named?","choices":["The Kirby-Bauer test and MIC strips","The tuberculin skin test and allergy testing","Blood typing and cross-matching","The Western blot and complement fixation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, both introducing antigen into the body to determine whether antibodies are present rather than testing serum in a tube. — PAJ5200.Diagnosing Infections-2.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"What do immunological methods take advantage of?","choices":["Antigen-antibody specificity","Differences in growth requirements","Variation in ribosomal gene sequence","The pH change produced by fermentation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and they may or may not require culturing — which places them between the phenotypic methods, which need growth, and the genotypic ones, which do not. — PAJ5200.Diagnosing Infections-2.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What do universal precautions achieve?","choices":["They eliminate the risk of transmission entirely","They sterilise the specimen before handling","They are needed only for known infectious specimens","They mitigate but do not eliminate transmission risk"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, which is why they are paired with aseptic procedure rather than relied on alone. Gloves and face shields or masks are the named examples. — PAJ5200.Diagnosing Infections-2.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What has largely replaced the complement fixation test?","choices":["The tuberculin skin test","The enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay","The Gram stain","Disk-diffusion susceptibility testing"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, which is unsurprising given that the older test needs four components and produces a reading that runs opposite to intuition. Similar methods too. — PAJ5200.Diagnosing Infections-2.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"What is the Western blot used to verify?","choices":["Blood group compatibility","HIV status, as a second test","Streptococcal pharyngitis","Tuberculosis exposure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it has also been used definitively for bovine spongiform encephalopathy, Lyme disease and hepatitis B. — PAJ5200.Diagnosing Infections-2.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"How do agglutination and precipitation reactions compare in sensitivity?","choices":["Agglutination is generally more sensitive","Precipitation is generally more sensitive","They are equally sensitive","Neither is sensitive enough for diagnosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it is also more practical — it can be run on a plastic card or glass slide, and the colour-changing rapid strep tests are a modified agglutination technique. — PAJ5200.Diagnosing Infections-2.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"For which organism is animal inoculation given as an example?","choices":["Legionella","Streptococcus pyogenes","Treponema pallidum","Mycobacterium leprae"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and it belongs with phage typing among the phenotypic techniques used when ordinary culture will not serve. — PAJ5200.Diagnosing Infections-2.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What is a titre?","choices":["The amount of antibody in serum","The concentration of antigen in tissue","The minimum concentration of drug that inhibits growth","The number of colonies on a plate"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, often reported in binding antibody units — so a serological test can quantify as well as identify. — PAJ5200.Diagnosing Infections-2.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"What kind of technique are the colour-changing rapid strep tests?","choices":["A modified agglutination technique","A precipitation reaction in gel","An enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay","A genotypic amplification method"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, which places them with blood typing and the latex pregnancy test rather than with the precipitation methods. — PAJ5200.Diagnosing Infections-2.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Which category of method usually does NOT require culturing?","choices":["All three require culture","Genotypic","Phenotypic","Immunological"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, which is the whole practical point of it — slow growers such as Mycobacterium and difficult ones such as Legionella can be identified without waiting for growth. — PAJ5200.Diagnosing Infections-2.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What is serology?","choices":["In vitro diagnostic testing of serum samples","In vivo introduction of antigen into the body","Culture of an organism on selective media","Amplification of nucleic acid from a specimen"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and because antibodies have extreme specificity for antigens, these are very sensitive methods. — PAJ5200.Diagnosing Infections-2.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"What does the Kirby-Bauer disk-diffusion test give you?","choices":["The antibody titre in the patient's serum","Which drug is effective, and at what dose","The identity of the organism","The genetic basis of any resistance"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it may also indicate which combinations of drugs can be used — so one plate informs both agent choice and dosing. — PAJ5200.Diagnosing Infections-2.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What does a blue colour indicate on Simmons citrate medium?","choices":["A falling pH from fermentation","Haemolysis of the red cells in the medium","Production of hydrogen sulphide","A rising pH"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, reported by the bromthymol blue indicator — citrate utilisation drives the pH up and the indicator reports it. — PAJ5200.Diagnosing Infections-2.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Who is credited with inventing the basic concept of the polymerase chain reaction?","choices":["Kary Mullis","Edwin Southern","Robert Koch","Kirby and Bauer"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The technique amplifies specific stretches of DNA or RNA and underpins much of modern genotypic diagnosis. He worked with colleagues on it. — PAJ5200.Diagnosing Infections-2.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which four components does the complement fixation test use?","choices":["Antigen, antibody, enzyme and chromogenic substrate","Primers, nucleotides, polymerase and template","Agar, antigen, antibody and a gel matrix","Antigen, antibody, complement and sensitised sheep red cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The antibody is referred to as lysin, and the technique is described as lysin-mediated haemolysis. — PAJ5200.Diagnosing Infections-2.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"What can immunoelectrophoresis identify?","choices":["Specific antibody classes binding antigen in serum","The species of an unknown bacterium","The minimum inhibitory concentration of a drug","Specific DNA sequences in a sample"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — IgG, IgM and the rest — and the technique is run on agarose or a similar gelling material. — PAJ5200.Diagnosing Infections-2.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"How should all specimens be regarded?","choices":["As hazardous only if they contain blood","As potentially infectious","As safe once fixed or preserved","As safe if the organism is known to be non-pathogenic"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, which is why aseptic procedures and universal precautions such as gloves and face shields are used — they mitigate but do not eliminate the risk. — PAJ5200.Diagnosing Infections-2.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"Why is testing for antibodies often preferred to testing for the microbe itself?","choices":["It does not require a specimen","It identifies antimicrobial susceptibility at the same time","It works without knowing what is suspected","It is generally easier and more accurate"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the rapid strep test takes ten to fifteen minutes, against the far longer wait for a culture to grow. — PAJ5200.Diagnosing Infections-2.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What is blood agar used to detect?","choices":["Salt tolerance","Urease activity","Haemolytic activity","Anaerobic growth"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, whereas chocolate agar is used mainly for anaerobic culturing — two plates that look similar and answer different questions. — PAJ5200.Diagnosing Infections-2.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which of these uses agglutination?","choices":["The Ouchterlony double-diffusion method","The complement fixation test","The enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay","The Weil-Felix test for rickettsial diseases"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, alongside blood typing, rapid syphilis diagnosis, cold agglutinin for mycoplasmas and the latex-agglutination pregnancy test. — PAJ5200.Diagnosing Infections-2.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"What does the Western blot detect?","choices":["Lipid and carbohydrate epitopes","Proteins","DNA sequences","RNA, to study gene expression"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — electrophoretic separation followed by an immunoassay, with sites of specific antibody binding appearing as a pattern of bands. — PAJ5200.Diagnosing Infections-2.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Why may phenotypic methods take longer to reach a diagnosis?","choices":["The microbes must be cultured","The equipment is slower to operate","They need a specialist to interpret them","They require a second confirmatory specimen"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — observable characteristics, biochemical reactions and sensitivity testing all depend on having grown the organism first. — PAJ5200.Diagnosing Infections-2.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"What does a hot pink colour change in urea broth indicate?","choices":["Citrate utilisation","Beta haemolysis","The presence of urease","Fermentation of mannitol"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — urea hydrolysis raises the pH, and the indicator reports the rise as a strong pink. — PAJ5200.Diagnosing Infections-2.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which ribosomal RNA gene is used for prokaryotes?","choices":["28S","5S","16S","18S"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and 18S is used for eukaryotes. Comparing base sequences establishes relationships and allows phylogenetic trees to be prepared. — PAJ5200.Diagnosing Infections-2.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What does agglutination testing detect?","choices":["Proteins separated by electrophoresis","Specific DNA sequences","Whole-cell or insoluble antigens","Soluble antigens made insoluble by antibody"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — antibody cross-links them into complexes that settle out as large visible clumps, generally sinking to the bottom of the container. — PAJ5200.Diagnosing Infections-2.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"What is G plus C content useful for?","choices":["Measuring antibody titre","Determining antimicrobial susceptibility","Taxonomic determination","Precisely identifying the species every time"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the qualifier matters, because a sequence statistic can place an organism broadly without naming it. It does not necessarily give a specific identification. — PAJ5200.Diagnosing Infections-2.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What are immunoassays especially good at?","choices":["Determining antimicrobial susceptibility","Growing slow-growing organisms rapidly","Constructing phylogenetic trees","Detecting trace amounts of antigen"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — they are extremely sensitive, which is what makes them suitable when the quantity present is very small. Trace antibody too. — PAJ5200.Diagnosing Infections-2.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"What do results of specimen analysis depend on?","choices":["The antibiotic the patient has received","The age of the patient","Collection, handling, transport and storage","The identification method chosen alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and requirements vary — some specimens need a preservative or fixative, others cooling, heating or another special treatment. — PAJ5200.Diagnosing Infections-2.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What happens to the clumps formed in an agglutination reaction?","choices":["They migrate through a gel under current","They settle to the bottom of the container","They remain suspended and are read by absorbance","They dissolve once complement is added"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, which is what makes the result visible without instrumentation and lets the test run on a card or slide. — PAJ5200.Diagnosing Infections-2.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"What produces the signal in an ELISA?","choices":["Emission from a fluorescent dye","Haemolysis of sensitised red cells","An enzyme-substrate reaction","Decay of a radioactive isotope"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, from an enzyme-antibody complex. The 96-well plates and eight-channel rapid pipettors make the format practical at scale. It yields a coloured chromogen. — PAJ5200.Diagnosing Infections-2.pptx, Slide 50"}]};
